---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import S from '../components/S.astro';
import T from '../components/T.astro';
import L from '../components/L.astro';
import Cite from '../components/Cite.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import Deeper from '../components/Deeper.astro';
import Widget from '../components/Widget.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Collectives on the Wire"
  description="An all-reduce that reduces inside the switch ASIC instead of at the endpoints stops generating most of its own traffic — the trick NVSwitch plays in the rack, played again one tier out."
  heading="Collectives on the Wire"
  slug="/sharp"
>
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="lede">
      Everything so far has been about carrying bytes faster or more reliably. This chapter is
      about not carrying them at all: if the switch can do the arithmetic as the data passes
      through, most of the traffic a collective would have generated never exists.
    </p>

    <h2 id="allreduce">The operation that dominates</h2>

    <L at={1}>
      <p>
        Training works like this: every GPU computes a suggested adjustment to the model, and then
        all of them have to agree on the sum of those suggestions before anyone can continue. That
        agreement is called an all-reduce, and it happens constantly.
      </p>
      <p>
        The obvious way to do it is to pass partial totals around a circle until everyone has seen
        everything, which means the same numbers cross the network many times. The clever way is
        to let the network add them up on the way past — so each GPU sends its contribution once,
        and gets the answer back.
      </p>
    </L>

    <L min={2}>
      <p>
        An all-reduce combines one value per participant and delivers the result to all of them.
        Its cost is the reason interconnect matters at all in training, and there are three ways
        to pay it.
      </p>
      <p>
        A <strong>ring</strong> passes partial sums around a cycle: bandwidth-optimal in total
        bytes but with a step count proportional to the number of participants, so latency grows
        linearly. A <strong>tree</strong> reduces up and broadcasts down in logarithmic steps —
        far better latency, at the cost of more total traffic. Both do the arithmetic at the
        endpoints, which means every intermediate result travels the fabric.
      </p>
      <p>
        <T id="sharp">SHARP</T> does it in the switch. Partial results are combined inside the
        ASIC as they pass through, so an endpoint sends its contribution once and receives the
        answer once, and the intermediate values never appear on the wire at all.
      </p>
    </L>

    <L min={3}>
      <p>
        The point worth carrying is that this is a <em>traffic elimination</em> mechanism, not a
        transport optimisation. Ring and tree both move the same data repeatedly and differ only
        in how they schedule it; in-network reduction changes how many bytes exist. That is why it
        composes with everything else rather than competing with it.
      </p>
      <p>
        It composes with itself, too. <a href="/nvlink#sharp">Chapter 5 of Part I</a> describes
        SHARP engines inside the in-rack NVSwitch; Quantum-X800 carries
        <S id="sharp-v4" /> on the scale-out switch.<Cite ids="nv-quantum-x800" /> A cluster-wide
        all-reduce can therefore reduce within each rack over NVLink first and then across racks
        over InfiniBand — two tiers of in-network reduction, so what crosses the expensive tier is
        one value per rack rather than one per GPU.
      </p>
    </L>
  </div>

  <Widget
    title="All-reduce cost"
    hint="Scale it up · switch the algorithm"
    caption="Standard first-order cost models, measured as bytes each GPU puts on the wire and dependent steps it waits on: ring is 2(N−1)/N × payload over 2(N−1) steps, tree is payload × log₂N over 2·log₂N steps, and in-network reduction is one payload over two steps. Real implementations pipeline, chunk and overlap, so treat these as ratios and growth rates rather than predictions — which is where the argument lives anyway."
  >
    <div data-allreduce>
      <svg class="diagram" viewBox="0 0 720 250" role="img"
        aria-label="A comparison of ring, tree and in-network all-reduce, showing bytes crossing the fabric and step count as the number of participating GPUs grows.">
        <g data-plot></g>
      </svg>
      <dl class="readout" style="margin-top:0.75rem">
        <div><dt>Participants</dt><dd data-out="n">576</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Payload each</dt><dd data-out="payload">256 MB</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Algorithm</dt><dd data-out="algo">In-network</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Bytes per GPU</dt><dd data-out="bytes">512 MB</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Steps</dt><dd data-out="steps">2</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Versus ring</dt><dd data-out="ratio">1.0×</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p data-out="verdict" style="margin:0.9rem 0 0;font-size:0.875rem;color:var(--ink-2)"></p>
    </div>
    <div slot="controls" class="controls-row">
      <div class="ctl">
        <label for="n">Participating GPUs<output data-out="n-lbl">576</output></label>
        <input type="range" id="n" data-n min="0" max="100" value="62" />
      </div>
      <div class="ctl">
        <label for="pl">Payload per GPU<output data-out="pl-lbl">256 MB</output></label>
        <input type="range" id="pl" data-pl min="0" max="100" value="50" />
      </div>
      <div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="Algorithm">
        <button type="button" data-algo="ring" aria-pressed="false">Ring</button>
        <button type="button" data-algo="tree" aria-pressed="false">Tree</button>
        <button type="button" data-algo="sharp" aria-pressed="true">In-network</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Widget>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2 id="cost">What it costs the switch</h2>

    <L min={2}>
      <p>
        In-network reduction is not free silicon. <a href="/nvlink#sharp">Part I</a> makes the
        point about NVSwitch: an <S id="nvswitch-asic" /> ASIC looks underspecified beside a raw
        51.2 Tb/s switch of the same era, and the difference is silicon that could have been ports
        and went to reduction engines instead.
      </p>
      <p>
        The same trade is made on the scale-out switch. You are buying arithmetic units, buffering
        and the control logic to build aggregation trees, and paying for them in port count or die
        area. It is a good trade exactly when collectives dominate your traffic — which is the
        defining property of this workload and not of most others.
      </p>
    </L>

    <L min={3}>
      <p>
        There is an operational dependency here worth naming. An aggregation tree has to be
        constructed over the physical topology before it can be used, which requires something
        that knows the topology — the <a href="/routing#sm">subnet manager</a>. In-network
        reduction is therefore downstream of centralised routing: it is one of the capabilities
        that a single authority over the fabric buys, and it is hard to offer on a fabric where
        nobody has the whole map.
      </p>
      <p>
        <T id="nccl">NCCL</T> is what actually selects among ring, tree and SHARP, from the
        topology it discovers. Almost nothing above it makes that choice explicitly, which is why
        fabric decisions surface to model engineers as throughput rather than as code — and why
        a fabric that reports its topology accurately is worth more than one that merely runs
        fast.
      </p>
    </L>

    <Callout kind="key">
      <p>
        Ring optimises total bytes. Tree optimises step count. In-network reduction changes how
        many bytes there are. That is why it is the only one of the three whose advantage
        <em>grows</em> with the size of the job rather than trading against something else.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <Callout kind="myth">
      <p>
        <strong>"SHARP is an NVLink feature."</strong> It is a switch feature, and both tiers of
        switch have it. The site said so only about NVSwitch until this chapter, which understated
        it: <S id="sharp-v4" /> runs on the Quantum-X800 scale-out switch too, and the two compose
        — reduce within the rack over NVLink, then across racks over InfiniBand.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <Deeper title="Why this changes what crosses the expensive tier">
      <p>
        Put the two tiers together with the numbers from
        <a href="/scale-out">chapter 7 of Part I</a>. Inside a rack a GPU has
        <S id="nvlink-egress" /> of egress; across racks it has <S id="cx8-egress" /> at best. The
        scale-out tier is the scarce one by nine to eighteen times.
      </p>
      <p>
        Reduce within each rack first and 72 contributions collapse to one before anything crosses
        that boundary. For a 16-rack job that is 1,152 GPU contributions reduced to 16 rack
        contributions on the expensive tier. The in-rack reduction is nearly free because it
        happens where bandwidth is abundant; the expensive tier carries a seventy-second of what
        it otherwise would. This is the clearest case on the site of two independent design
        decisions — a coherent rack domain and an in-network-reduction fabric — multiplying rather
        than merely adding.
      </p>
    </Deeper>
  </div>

  <script>
    const root = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-allreduce]');
    if (root) {
      const fig = root.closest('figure')!;
      const NS = 'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg';
      const plot = root.querySelector('[data-plot]')!;
      const out = (k: string) => fig.querySelector<HTMLElement>(`[data-out="${k}"]`)!;
      const nSlider = fig.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('[data-n]')!;
      const plSlider = fig.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('[data-pl]')!;
      const el = (n: string, a: Record<string, string | number>) => {
        const e = document.createElementNS(NS, n);
        for (const k in a) e.setAttribute(k, String(a[k]));
        return e;
      };
      let algo = 'sharp';

      const nOf = () => Math.round(8 * Math.pow(9216 / 8, Number(nSlider.value) / 100));
      const plOf = () => 1e6 * Math.pow(8000, Number(plSlider.value) / 100);
      const fmtB = (b: number) => b >= 1e9 ? (b / 1e9).toFixed(2) + ' GB' : (b / 1e6).toFixed(0) + ' MB';

      /*
       * Standard first-order collective cost models. The metric is bytes each
       * GPU puts on the wire, and the number of dependent steps it waits on.
       *
       *   ring  — 2(N-1)/N · S bytes over 2(N-1) steps: bandwidth-optimal
       *           among endpoint algorithms, latency proportional to N.
       *   tree  — S·log2(N) bytes over 2·log2(N) steps: trades bandwidth for
       *           latency, because intermediate results cross the fabric again.
       *   SHARP — S bytes over 2 steps: the contribution goes up once and the
       *           result comes back, and no intermediate value is ever sent.
       */
      const models = (N: number, S: number) => ({
        ring: { bytes: 2 * ((N - 1) / N) * S, steps: Math.max(1, 2 * (N - 1)), label: 'Ring' },
        tree: { bytes: S * Math.log2(Math.max(2, N)), steps: Math.max(2, Math.ceil(2 * Math.log2(N))), label: 'Tree' },
        sharp: { bytes: S, steps: 2, label: 'In-network' },
      });

      const render = () => {
        const N = nOf(), S = plOf();
        const m = models(N, S);
        const cur = m[algo as keyof typeof m];

        out('n').textContent = N.toLocaleString();
        out('n-lbl').textContent = N.toLocaleString();
        out('payload').textContent = fmtB(S);
        out('pl-lbl').textContent = fmtB(S);
        out('algo').textContent = cur.label;
        out('bytes').textContent = fmtB(cur.bytes);
        out('steps').textContent = cur.steps.toLocaleString();
        out('ratio').textContent = (cur.bytes / m.ring.bytes).toFixed(2) + '×';

        out('verdict').textContent = algo === 'sharp'
          ? `At ${N.toLocaleString()} GPUs the in-network reduction still takes two steps and one payload per GPU, because neither depends on how many participants there are. That flatness is the whole point: ring needs ${m.ring.steps.toLocaleString()} steps at this scale, and tree ${(m.tree.bytes / m.sharp.bytes).toFixed(1)}× the bytes.`
          : algo === 'ring'
            ? `Ring moves the least data of the two endpoint algorithms, but its ${m.ring.steps.toLocaleString()} dependent steps at ${N.toLocaleString()} GPUs mean latency grows linearly with the job — and it still sends ${(m.ring.bytes / m.sharp.bytes).toFixed(1)}× what in-network reduction sends.`
            : `Tree cuts the step count to ${m.tree.steps} by reducing up and broadcasting down, but every intermediate result crosses the fabric on the way — ${(m.tree.bytes / m.ring.bytes).toFixed(1)}× ring's bytes at this scale. It trades bandwidth for latency rather than removing either.`;

        // ── Draw: bytes and steps side by side ────────────────────────
        plot.textContent = '';
        const keys = ['ring', 'tree', 'sharp'] as const;

        const byteMax = Math.max(...keys.map((k) => m[k].bytes));
        const stepMax = Math.max(...keys.map((k) => m[k].steps));

        const heading = (x: number, t: string) => {
          const e = el('text', { x, y: 14, fill: 'var(--ink-3)' });
          e.textContent = t;
          plot.appendChild(e);
        };
        heading(96, 'Bytes on the wire per GPU');
        heading(430, 'Dependent steps (log scale)');

        keys.forEach((k, i) => {
          const y = 40 + i * 56;
          const on = k === algo;
          const name = el('text', { x: 88, y: y + 14, 'text-anchor': 'end',
            fill: on ? 'var(--copper)' : 'var(--ink-3)' });
          name.textContent = m[k].label;
          plot.appendChild(name);

          plot.appendChild(el('rect', { x: 96, y, width: Math.max(2, (m[k].bytes / byteMax) * 220),
            height: 20, rx: 3, fill: on ? 'var(--copper)' : 'var(--line-strong)',
            opacity: on ? 0.9 : 0.45 }));
          const bl = el('text', { x: 102 + (m[k].bytes / byteMax) * 220, y: y + 14 });
          bl.textContent = fmtB(m[k].bytes);
          plot.appendChild(bl);

          // Steps span four orders of magnitude between algorithms, so a linear
          // bar would render in-network as nothing at all.
          const w = (Math.log10(m[k].steps) / Math.log10(Math.max(10, stepMax))) * 200;
          plot.appendChild(el('rect', { x: 430, y, width: Math.max(2, w), height: 20, rx: 3,
            fill: on ? 'var(--gpu)' : 'var(--line-strong)', opacity: on ? 0.9 : 0.45 }));
          const sl = el('text', { x: 436 + Math.max(2, w), y: y + 14 });
          sl.textContent = m[k].steps.toLocaleString();
          plot.appendChild(sl);
        });

        const foot = el('text', { x: 360, y: 226, 'text-anchor': 'middle', fill: 'var(--ink-3)' });
        foot.textContent = `${N.toLocaleString()} GPUs · ${fmtB(S)} each`;
        plot.appendChild(foot);
      };

      nSlider.addEventListener('input', render);
      plSlider.addEventListener('input', render);
      fig.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-algo]').forEach((b) => {
        b.addEventListener('click', () => {
          algo = b.dataset.algo!;
          fig.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-algo]').forEach((o) =>
            o.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(o === b)));
          render();
        });
      });
      render();
    }
  </script>
</Base>
